%9Syntax:%9

@SYNTAX:update@

%9Parameters:%9

    CHECK:       Asks now whether a newer signed release is there, and
                 shows the answer (also when nothing is newer, and why a
                 check failed).
    INSTALL:     Installs the newest signed release, if it is newer than
                 this erssi, in the background.
    -upgrade:    With INSTALL: runs /UPGRADE once it is installed.

    Without an argument: whether update_check is on, when the last check
    was made and what it found, and whether an install is running.

%9Description:%9

    Tells you about new releases of erssi Shellter Edition, with a short
    summary of what a new one brings when its release tag has one.

    The check runs the installer that was installed with this erssi
    (shellter-install.sh --update --check --porcelain, the same check as
    erssi --check-update) in the background, so erssi goes on while it
    runs. The installer asks the repository it installed from (GitHub by
    default) for the release tags and takes the newest one with a valid
    signature by the release key it carries; a tag signed by anyone else
    is not counted. It downloads the tag list and one tag with its commit
    (about 6 KB; one more such tag for each newer one it skips, five at
    most), never the files of a release. One check runs at a time,
    and one that takes longer than two minutes is stopped.

    Nothing is installed by a check. Only /UPDATE INSTALL installs, and
    only when you give it: it checks first, then runs the same installer
    with --update (as erssi --update in a shell does), in the background
    and without a terminal, so it asks nothing. erssi goes on while it
    runs. Its steps, warnings and errors are shown in the Notices window;
    everything it prints goes to ~/.erssi/update-install.log (mode 0600).
    A ready-made package installs in seconds, a build from source can
    take 20 minutes or more; one that takes longer than an hour is
    stopped. Nothing is installed when this erssi is the newest release,
    one install runs at a time (the installer locks the install, and the
    log is locked for another erssi with the same home), and erssi does
    not run another tool named as the updater of the install (a system
    package): update such an erssi the way it was installed. As root,
    missing system packages stop the install (erssi --update in a shell
    asks before installing them).

    The running erssi is not changed: /UPGRADE loads the new release and
    keeps your windows and channels; TLS connections reconnect once.
    When erssi was not started as the install's bin/erssi (or a link to
    it), the message names the binary to give /UPGRADE. /QUIT or
    /UPGRADE while an install runs stops it; the installer leaves the
    release in use as it was.

    erssi checks by itself only with update_check on (/HELP update_check).
    An erssi that the installer did not install (a distribution package,
    a build of your own) has nothing to run: the check says so, and you
    update it the way it was installed. In Capsicum capability mode no
    program can be started, so there is no check and no install either;
    erssi --check-update and erssi --update from a shell still work.

%9Examples:%9

    /UPDATE
    /UPDATE CHECK
    /UPDATE INSTALL
    /UPDATE INSTALL -upgrade
    /SET update_check ON

%9See also:%9 UPGRADE, UPDATE_CHECK, STATUSBAR

